  <prompt>
    The Association Engine (`IncomingAssociationResolver`) is a DI singleton. Its private
    `_recordTypeRefCache` was a plain `Dictionary&lt;string,(Guid,string)?&gt;` mutated from
    `ResolveRecordTypeRefAsync` on the request thread. Inbound (and, once 017 wires it, outbound)
    communications resolve on parallel threads, so concurrent writes to the plain dictionary are a data
    race (torn state / lost writes / rare corruption). Swap it to a `ConcurrentDictionary`. The
    read-then-query-then-write is intentionally left non-atomic: the cached value is deterministic per
    key, so a rare double-query just re-writes the same entry — no lock needed, no corruption.
  </prompt>

  <goal>
    `_recordTypeRefCache` is a `ConcurrentDictionary`; the singleton resolver is safe under concurrent
    ResolveAsync calls. Build clean; existing Communication tests (which exercise resolver-field
    population) still pass; no behavior change.
  </goal>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">`_recordTypeRefCache` is a `ConcurrentDictionary&lt;string,(Guid,string)?&gt;`.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">`dotnet build` clean; Communication test suite green (no regression).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">No behavior change — cache semantics (lazy populate, null-negative caching) preserved.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>
    Owner instruction (2026-07-15): resolve the 015 code-review thread-safety Warning IN THIS PROJECT
    (fold into 016/017 or a new task), NOT via a deferred issue. Filed as its own task for traceability.
    A deterministic concurrency unit test would be flaky scaffolding (ADR-038 B10) — the fix is correct by
    the ConcurrentDictionary type guarantee and is covered indirectly by the existing resolver-field tests.
  </notes>
</task>
